<hr data-start="284" data-end="287">
<h1 data-start="289" data-end="374">Aspect Relegation Theory: A Relevance–Transjective Account of Cognitive Compression</h1>
<h2 data-start="376" data-end="387">Abstract</h2>
<p data-start="388" data-end="1081"><em data-start="388" data-end="420">Aspect Relegation Theory (ART)</em> formalizes the process by which cognitive aspects—initially represented as high-gradient, high-relevance operations—undergo dynamic flattening of their relevance fields and become delegated to stable transjective morphisms. Through the combined structure of <strong data-start="679" data-end="716">Relevance Activation Theory (RAT)</strong> and the <strong data-start="725" data-end="767">Amplitwistor/Transjective Theory (ATT)</strong>, ART defines cognitive automatization as a transition from cue-sensitive gradient flows to endofunctorial idempotence across coupled semantic–motor categories. The theory thereby unifies proceduralization, habit formation, and automaticity as entropic relaxations in the joint manifold of relevance and resonance.</p>
<hr data-start="1083" data-end="1086">
<h2 data-start="1088" data-end="1115">1. Conceptual Foundation</h2>
<h3 data-start="1117" data-end="1165">1.1 RAT Basis: Relevance Gradient Flattening</h3>
<p data-start="1166" data-end="1346">Under <strong data-start="1172" data-end="1179">RAT</strong>, cognition operates as gradient ascent over scalar <em data-start="1231" data-end="1249">relevance fields</em> <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant="double-struck">R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R: X \to \mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">:</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6889em;"></span><span class="mord mathbb">R</span></span></span></span>, where <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span></span></span></span> denotes perceptual–motor state space. Behavior follows:</p>
