</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{document}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\maketitle</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vspace</span></span><span>{-0.5em}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{abstract}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\noindent</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Aspect Relegation Theory (ART)} formalizes the process by which
cognitive aspects---initially represented as high-gradient, cue-sensitive operations---
undergo dynamic flattening of their relevance fields and become delegated to
stable transjective morphisms. Integrating </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Relevance Activation Theory (RAT)}
and the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Amplitwistor--Transjective Theory (ATT)}, ART defines cognitive automatization
as a convergence from cue-driven gradient flow to idempotent endofunctorial closure.
The result is a unified account of habit, automaticity, and cognitive compression
as thermodynamic relaxation across coupled semantic and motor manifolds.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{abstract}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Conceptual Overview}

When a behavior or cognitive procedure transitions from deliberate control
to automatic execution, its phenomenological salience decreases while
its stability and energetic efficiency increase. We designate this process as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{aspect relegation}---the demotion of an initially conscious, high-relevance operation
into a stable, low-relevance attractor. Within the formal languages of RAT and ATT,
this phenomenon is captured as the joint limit
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> R(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> 0, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Id},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the cue-indexed relevance field and
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T = G </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a transjective endofunctor describing
reciprocal inference between semantic and motor categories.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Relevance Activation Basis (RAT)}

RAT models cognition as gradient ascent on scalar relevance fields
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R : X </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> defined over perceptual or motor spaces~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{rat}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{dx}{dt} = f(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> R(x), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:ratflow}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denotes the transition dynamics.
Cue activations are Gaussian-localized perturbations
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>c(x) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>x - x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>) w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>c,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
and synaptic trails reinforce recurrent paths
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ij} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span> A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
An aspect </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> becomes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{relegated} when its local relevance curvature
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> asymptotically vanishes:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lim</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> = 0,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
indicating that additional cue activations no longer change behavior.
RAT thus describes relegation as a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{gradient quenching process}:
the energy stored in predictive discrepancy is dissipated
through repeated traversal of a stable affordance field.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Transjective Closure Basis (ATT)}

In ATT, cognition is represented as a pair of adjoint functors
linking semantic and embodied categories:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T = G </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> F : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\longrightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\longrightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Here </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> projects semantic intention into embodied mode selection,
and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> re-integrates the embodied feedback into the semantic manifold.
The composite </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> acts as a self-updating operator on cognition.
Repeated application
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n+1} = T(X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n) = G(F(X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n))
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
defines a learning loop. Relegation corresponds to </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{endofunctorial idempotence},
the condition </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 = T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, signifying that further iterations yield no change.
The residual error </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n = X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n+1}-X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> vanishes as the system
achieves perfect closure between intention and enactment.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Relevance--Resonance Functional}

The convergence of RAT and ATT can be formalized
by a relevance--resonance functional </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}[x]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}[x] =
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> R(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 +
D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{FR}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{*} g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 , g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:Lfunctional}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{FR}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the Fisher--Rao divergence between
the pulled-back metric </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{*} g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and the intrinsic semantic metric </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
Learning corresponds to gradient descent on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}{dt} &lt; 0, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lim</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}[x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t] = 0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
In this terminal condition, both the relevance gradient
and the transjective mismatch vanish.
Relegation thus represents the joint zero-curvature limit:
a cognitive manifold of minimum free energy and maximal coherence.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Neurodynamic Realization}

Within the ATT neurodynamic framework,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Central Pattern Generator} (CPG) chains implement
the mapping </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
Each mode </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> corresponds to a semantic projection vector </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
acting as a filter </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>P</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t} = v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t} v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\top</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> on cognitive state </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
The temporal update is non-Markovian:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1} = f</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(
P</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t} x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t +
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{T} K(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>) P</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>}} x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> encodes decaying memory of past modes.
Aspect relegation occurs when </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and a single limit-cycle
attractor stabilizes, converting deliberate control into rhythmic automation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Amplitwistor condensation.}
In the amplitwistor formalism, cortical oscillations are represented
as </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(Z,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span>{Z},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{amp}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
Relegation corresponds to phase condensation:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dim</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{active}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\downarrow</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\arg</span></span><span>(Z</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span>{Z})}{dt}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> 0,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
reflecting the synchronization of neural phases and the reduction
of information-theoretic curvature in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{amp}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Phenomenological Implications}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Automation and Habit}
At the phenomenological level, relegation manifests as automation.
Attention---represented in RAT as a vector field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vec</span></span><span>{A}:C</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> TC</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
guiding traversal of cue space---collapses to zero when all local gradients vanish.
The aspect becomes backgrounded: executed flawlessly yet no longer
accessible to introspective modulation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Cognitive Economy}
Relegation conserves thermodynamic work
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
W = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span> |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> R|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 dt,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
minimizing the energetic expenditure of maintaining high-curvature cognition.
It thereby implements a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{principle of entropic efficiency}:
the brain reallocates free energy to regions of persistent novelty.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{De--Relegation}
When environmental perturbations restore curvature to the relevance field
(</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>), idempotence breaks.
The endofunctor reopens, re-engaging the transjective learning loop.
This cycle---relegation and de-relegation---constitutes the dynamic
equilibrium of cognitive economy and adaptability.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Discussion}

Aspect Relegation Theory thus unifies proceduralization,
habit formation, and cognitive compression under a single geometric
and thermodynamic principle. Within RAT, relegation is gradient quenching;
within ATT, it is transjective closure.
At equilibrium, the system's internal manifold becomes flat,
its oscillatory substrate phase-locked, and its categorical morphisms idempotent.
Cognition thereby achieves a state of minimal surprise
without external mediation---the silent competence of an
autonomous relational engine.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Acknowledgments}
This paper builds upon the Relevance Activation Theory (2025) and
Amplitwistor--Transjective Theory (2025) frameworks, extending their
integration under the Flyxion cognitive research program.

