</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\noindent</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Aspect Relegation Theory (ART)} formalizes the process by which
cognitive aspects---initially represented as high-gradient, cue-sensitive operations---
undergo dynamic flattening of their relevance fields and become delegated to
stable transjective morphisms. Integrating </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Relevance Activation Theory (RAT)},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Amplitwistor--Transjective Theory (ATT)}, and the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Epistemic Energy Functional}
of RSVP, ART defines cognitive automatization as a convergence from
cue-driven gradient flow to idempotent endofunctorial closure. The theory extends to
semantic agents and large language models, describing representation consolidation
as a thermodynamic form of aspect relegation.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{abstract}

