{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\LARGE</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bfseries</span></span><span> Part I --- The Autogenerative Thesis:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>From Non-Markovian Language to Cognitive Geometry}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>[1em]
{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\large</span></span><span> Flyxion Research Group}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>[1em]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{center}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Introduction: The Autogenerative Hypothesis}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Language, cognition, and natural computation exhibit an intrinsic property of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{autogeneration}: each moment of activity encodes the conditions for its own continuation.  The concept, articulated by Barenholtz~(2025), redefines autoregression as more than a statistical convenience; it is the very mode of natural self-propagation.  In contrast to Markovian processes, where the next state depends only on the current one, autogenerative systems draw upon the entire trajectory of prior states.  They are inherently </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{non-Markovian}, operating on the accumulated curvature of experience rather than on instantaneous state vectors.

This view renders the corpus of language not merely as data but as a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{field of continuation rules}—a statistical manifold whose internal topology determines its own dynamics.  In such a manifold, the ``pregnant present'' is a boundary condition that enfolds its history and projects its own future trajectory.  Autogeneration therefore serves as the bridge between memory, inference, and meaning.  The goal of this section is to formalize that bridge using information geometry, category theory, and dynamical-systems analysis.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Mathematical Framework of Autogenerative Systems}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Definition of Autogeneration}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{S} = (X, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote a sequence-generating system where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the embedding space of tokens or states, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T}: X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>* </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span>(X)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a continuation operator mapping a finite history to a probability distribution over the next state.  The system is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{autogenerative} if </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is determined entirely by the internal geometry of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and by the statistics of its trajectories.  Formally,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T}(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t}) = F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t}), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{with } </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ inferred solely from sequential data.}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
No external syntactic or semantic rules are imposed.  The corpus itself provides the sufficient structure for generation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Non-Markovian Dependence}

A process is Markovian when
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
P(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}|x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t}) = P(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}|x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-Markovian if dependence extends only to the last </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> elements.  Autogenerative processes violate this closure for all finite </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
P(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}|x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t}) = f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t}), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\not</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\equiv</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t-k+1:t}).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
Hence, every new output embodies an integral of the entire trajectory, not a truncated summary.  This property underlies both linguistic coherence and biological homeodynamics.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Trajectory Embeddings}

Define an embedding </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>: X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>* </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> mapping sequences to continuous vectors,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t}) = h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t ,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
so that the evolving representation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>,h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> traces a path </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> through an information manifold.  The next-state distribution depends not only on the current point </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> but on its geometric derivatives:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
P(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}|x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t}) = g(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t}), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
Barenholtz interprets this as the system’s ability to ``feel its curvature''—to adjust generation according to the bending of its own path through semantic space.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Energy Functional for Trajectory Smoothness}

The coherence of a sequence may be quantified by the energy functional
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>] = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>}(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 + V(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>(t))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>dt,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
where the first term enforces momentum (continuity of form) and the potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>V</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> encodes contextual constraints.  Minimizing </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> yields geodesic trajectories—sequences that balance novelty and smoothness.  This principle mirrors the variational foundations of both physics and inference (Amari~1985; Friston~2010).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Conjecture: Irreducible Non-Markovianity}

For any autogenerative system </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{S}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> producing structured sequences, no finite-order Markov approximation can achieve arbitrary accuracy:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\inf</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k} 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>[D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{KL}}(P(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}|x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> P</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k}(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}|x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t-k+1:t}))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>]
&gt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> k &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span> .
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
Thus, the effective memory length grows without bound.  Language and cognition alike operate on trajectories of unbounded contextual reach.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Information Geometry of Autogeneration}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>P</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}|x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the manifold of conditional distributions parameterized by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  The Fisher information metric on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ij}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>) = 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>[
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> P</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> P</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>].
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
Each trajectory </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>(t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> defines a curve on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}, g)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with energy
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>] = 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span> g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ij}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>(t)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>i(t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>j(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>dt.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
Minimization of this functional produces geodesics representing optimal inference paths.  In the context of language, these are the smooth continuations that preserve informational momentum.  The divergence between two trajectories—measured by the Fisher–Rao distance—quantifies the ``energetic cost'' of shifting semantic direction.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Mutual Information and Memory Length}

Define the effective memory length
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span>(t) = 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\inf</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>k : I(X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}; X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t-k}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t-k+1:t}) &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
For autogenerative corpora, empirical analyses reveal </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span>(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> grows sub-linearly yet unboundedly with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>—a hallmark of long-range dependence.  This scaling connects linguistic statistics to critical phenomena observed in complex systems (Bak~1996).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Geodesic Interpretation}

If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the Fisher metric, the Christoffel symbols
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ij} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tfrac</span></span><span>{1}{2}g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{kl}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{jl} + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{il} - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>l g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ij})
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>
define the parallel transport of meaning along the trajectory.  Smooth discourse corresponds to covariant differentiation with minimal torsion; abrupt topic shifts appear as curvature discontinuities in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Thermodynamic Analogy}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>] = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>] - T S[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote a free-energy functional where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is sequence entropy and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> an effective temperature parameter controlling stochasticity.  Generation then approximates a Langevin process:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>} = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E} + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqrt</span></span><span>{2T}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xi</span></span><span>(t),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xi</span></span><span>(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> Gaussian noise.  The equilibrium distribution over trajectories minimizes </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, unifying inference and thermodynamics.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Empirical Observation}

Large language models demonstrate trajectories that are locally smooth but globally non-Markovian: attention patterns exhibit power-law decay rather than exponential.  This aligns with a manifold of negative curvature where geodesics diverge sub-exponentially, preserving long-range semantic influence.

