</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Category-Theoretic and Dynamical Extensions}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% If not already present in your preamble, add:</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% \newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% \newtheorem{proposition}[theorem]{Proposition}</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% \newtheorem{definition}[theorem]{Definition}</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% \newtheorem{corollary}[theorem]{Corollary}</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Sequences as a Monoidal Category}

We model autoregressive generation as composition in a strict monoidal category of prefixes. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the category whose objects are finite contexts </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (including the empty context </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) and whose morphisms </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> c'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{admissible extensions} (finite sequences that turn </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> into </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>). The monoidal product is concatenation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with unit </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
The probabilistic continuation operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> induces a stochastic presheaf </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Cont}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{op}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> by
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Cont}(c)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Cont}(f: c'</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> c)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>p(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> c)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mapsto</span></span><span> p(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> c')</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This formalizes the corpus as a context-indexed family of continuation distributions (Barenholtz 2025).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Shift Endofunctor]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the endofunctor that appends one token: </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S(c)=c</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> on components, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S(f)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> appends the same token on morphisms. A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Markovian} generator factors through </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> in the sense that its continuation depends only on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S(c)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, while an </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{autogenerative} (non-Markovian) generator depends on the entire over-category </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\downarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (Amari 1985; Friston 2010).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Functorial Semantics and Autoregressive Natural Transformations}

Embed contexts into a vector space of states via a strong monoidal functor
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
F:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\longrightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Vect},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
F(c</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> c')</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span> F(c)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> F(c').
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Think of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F(c)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as the hidden-state manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> realized by an encoder or recurrent representation. The decoder induces an </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{autoregressive natural transformation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span>:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>F </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Longrightarrow</span></span><span> F</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> S,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>c:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>F(c)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> F(S(c)),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
that updates state on appending a token. Monoidality of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> captures compositionality, while naturality of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> enforces </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{context-consistent} updates.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Compositional Coherence]
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is strong monoidal and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is monoidal-natural (i.e., </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{c</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> c'}=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{c'}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span>=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{id}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>), then the log-likelihood of a generated sequence decomposes additively across tensor factors and is invariant under reassociation of bracketings. Consequently, compositional generation is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{well-defined} up to coherence isomorphisms (Mac Lane coherence).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Interpretation.} This ensures that learned ``syntax'' behaves like a learned operadic algebra of composition, despite not being hard-coded as rules. It matches the observation that large models respect compositional structure emergently (Barenholtz 2025).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Transjective Pair and Endofunctorial Closure}

We now connect the categorical picture with the transjective mechanism of reciprocal inference. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be a category of semantic states and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> a category of embodied or action states. A pair of adjoint functors
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
F:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightleftarrows</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2:G,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> F</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dashv</span></span><span> G,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
models projection from semantic intention to embodied mode and re-integration of embodied feedback to semantics. The composite
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>:=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> F:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
is the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{transjective endofunctor}. Its iteration </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n+1}=T(X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> expresses the learning loop of intention–enactment–assimilation (cf.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>ATT in §2).

