</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Topological and Measure-Theoretic Foundations}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Path Spaces, Filtrations, and Predictive Structure}

Autogenerative systems live not in state space but in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{path space}.  
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span> = X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{[0,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>)}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the space of measurable trajectories </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span> : [0,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, with canonical filtration
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>:=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>(s)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> s</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Autoregression is equivalent to conditioning over </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> rather than over a finite window:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
P(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This filtration captures the non-Markovian persistence structure characteristic of language and biological cognition (Barenholtz 2025).  
Let the predictive representation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> satisfy:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>],
