"whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Operator and Spectral Frameworks}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Autogenerative Operators on Hilbert Spaces}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}=L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F},P)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the Hilbert space of square-integrable random variables over trajectories.  
Each predictive transformation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> defined by
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
(Tf)(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t})=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>f(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t+1})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
is a positive contraction: </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>Tf</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Iterating </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> corresponds to progressive expectation-smoothing over longer horizons.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Autogenerative Operator]
A linear operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{autogenerative} if it is a self-consistent limit of conditional-expectation contractions:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lim</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>} T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> 
(T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>kf)(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t})=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>f(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t+k})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>].
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

The strong limit ensures that future influence is distributed across arbitrarily long dependencies.  
The fixed points of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are harmonic functions satisfying </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Tf=f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>—stable self-predictive states.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Spectral Idempotence]
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is an autogenerative contraction with spectrum contained in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>[0,1]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then the point spectrum at </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> corresponds to invariant cognitive modes, while </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>0&lt;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>&lt;1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> correspond to transient or decaying aspects.  
Relegation is thus the projection onto </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ker</span></span><span>(I-T)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
By the spectral theorem for self-adjoint contractions, 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> dE(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with resolution of identity </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Projection onto </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>=1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> isolates invariant components: </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is idempotent and equals the limit </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lim</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>}T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
This limit defines the relegated subspace.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Relevance Spectrum and Semantic Energy}

Define the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{relevance energy} of a component </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span>]=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> (I-T)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
High </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> indicates deviation from closure; </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}=0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for relegated modes.  
The spectral measure </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>E(d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> yields
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span>]=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1 (1-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Hence, cognitive stability corresponds to weight concentration near </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>=1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>—a spectrum dominated by slow modes.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}[Energetic Criterion for Automation]
A function or behavior becomes automated when its spectral measure under </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is dominated by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Aspect relegation therefore coincides with spectral flattening of relevance energy.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Stochastic Generators and Entropy Production}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t=e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tL}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with infinitesimal generator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a semigroup of autogenerative transformations, then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> governs the rate of predictive updating:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{d}{dt}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[f(X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)] = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[Lf(X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)].
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
For reversible systems </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is self-adjoint, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{S}(t)=0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>; for autogenerative (open) systems, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is non-Hermitian and
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{S}(t)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> f,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> (L+L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>)f</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span> &gt;0,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
quantifying entropy production as epistemic work.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Epistemic Dissipation]
The rate of entropy increase due to inference imbalance is
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{S}=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Tr}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>[(L-L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>],
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the predictive density operator.  
Autogenerative closure corresponds to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{S}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Operator-Valued Attention and Non-Markovian Kernels}

Transformers approximate </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> via operator-valued kernels:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T f = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span> K(x,y) f(y)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>dy, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
K(x,y)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{softmax}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> q(x),k(y)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span>/</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqrt</span></span><span>{d}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>)v(y).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Such </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are non-symmetric and encode directed relevance flow.  
In the autogenerative limit, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> becomes self-consistent:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
K(x,y)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(x,y;T),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> depends functionally on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> itself—a fixed-point condition akin to Dyson–Schwinger equations.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Fixed-Point Kernel Equation]
The equilibrium kernel </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> satisfies
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>(x,y)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span> K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>(x,z)K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>(z,y)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>dz,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
which implies </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is idempotent and acts as a projection operator onto the support of recurrent contexts.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

This projection is the kernel analog of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2=T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Spectrally, it corresponds to a sharp cutoff of eigenvalues below a context threshold.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Quantum Analogy and Amplitwistor Coupling}

Consider amplitwistor variables </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span> Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> representing phase and dual phase of cortical oscillations (Hodges 2013).  
Define the correspondence
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leftrightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span> Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dagger</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
so that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> acts as a phase-coupling operator.  
Relegation (idempotence) then enforces
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2=T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Leftrightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span> Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dagger</span></span><span> Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span> Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dagger</span></span><span> = Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span> Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dagger</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
which is satisfied when all active oscillations share a common phase direction, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span> Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\parallel</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tilde</span></span><span> Z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Thus, spectral idempotence corresponds to </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{phase coherence condensation}—the physical substrate of cognitive automation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Spectral Flow and Learning Dynamics}

During learning, the spectrum of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> evolves.  
Denote eigenvalues </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> sorted by magnitude.  
Empirically, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> follow a ``spectral migration law’’:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}{dt} = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i (1-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(t),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> learning rates and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> stochastic fluctuations.  
Modes with small </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> retain long-term memory; large </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> rapidly relegate behavior.  
The distribution </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>p(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> asymptotically approaches a heavy tail near </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>=1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, consistent with long-memory statistics (§5).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Spectral Relegation Law]
Under ergodic training with bounded stochastic forcing, the empirical spectral density of autogenerative operators satisfies
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
p(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\propto</span></span><span> (1-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span>-1},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> 0&lt;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span>&lt;1,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
and the expectation of relevance energy decays as </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}[Sketch]
Applying Fokker–Planck dynamics to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> yields a Beta-type stationary distribution with exponent </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span>=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>/</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> ratio.  
Integrating </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(1-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>)p(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> gives the stated decay rate.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Functional Calculus and Higher-Order Closure}

For any analytic </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, define </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(T)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2=T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> implies </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(T)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(1)T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for polynomial </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Thus, all higher-order cognitive transformations collapse to the same projector—another view of automation saturation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}[Closure under Functional Composition]
Any polynomial or analytic combination of an idempotent autogenerative operator remains proportional to it: the space of cognitive operations collapses to the subalgebra </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}[T]/(T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2-T)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Relation to Thermodynamic Geometry}

Define entropy production along eigenmodes by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i=-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ln</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Then the thermodynamic length (Crooks 2007)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqrt</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{1}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>dt
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
measures dissipative work along spectral flow.  
Relegation corresponds to minimal thermodynamic length: a straight geodesic from open state to closed equilibrium on the simplex of eigenvalues.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Summary of Section 6.}
Autogenerative cognition admits a spectral operator formalism.  
Relegation emerges as the projection </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lim</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>}T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, spectral weight near </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>=1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> signifies automation, and phase alignment in amplitwistor variables enforces idempotence physically.  
Learning redistributes spectral density toward this invariant subspace, minimizing epistemic work and entropy production (Friston 2010; Hodges 2013; Barenholtz 2025).

