</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Topological Dynamics and Recurrence}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Symbolic Dynamics and Subshifts}

Autogenerative language can be modeled as a subshift of the full shift </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span>=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{Z}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>+}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> under the left shift </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mapsto</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subseteq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the closure of admissible sequences under corpus constraints.  
Then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a topological dynamical system with invariant probability measures </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> encoding corpus statistics (Bowen 1975; Ruelle 1978).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Autogenerative Subshift]
A subshift </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{autogenerative} if, for some continuous evaluation functional </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{E}:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (e.g.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>embedding), the induced skew-product map
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
F:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>(x,h)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mapsto</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> x,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(h,x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> admits an attracting invariant set </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> whose projection to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is full measure for every </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

Thus, predictive states </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> enslave to recurrent language structure: learning builds an attractor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> representing stable long-horizon behavior.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Poincaré Recurrence for Semantic Modes]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be ergodic and let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subset</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be a measurable set of semantic states.  
For </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-a.e.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the trajectory </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(x,h)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> under </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> returns to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> infinitely often provided </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Leb}(B)&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> preserves </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> absolutely continuous.  
Hence, semantic modes recur with probability 1.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}[Idea]
Apply Poincaré recurrence to the product system </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d,F)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> under an invariant absolutely continuous product measure.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Conley Index and Morse Decomposition}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the continuous-time predictive flow on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> driven by autoregression.  
Conley theory decomposes the chain recurrent set into Morse components </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with a partial order describing admissible transitions (Conley 1978).  
For language, each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> corresponds to a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{semantic basin}; edges encode allowable large-scale narrative moves.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Conley Invariants of Discourse]
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>N</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is an isolating neighborhood of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the homotopy type of the index pair </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(N</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i,L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (the Conley index) is a topological invariant of the semantic basin.  
Stable aspect relegation collapses </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to a single fixed point in the index, reflecting automation of that basin.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Temporal Graphs and Recurrent Communities}

Define a time-varying graph </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t=(V,E</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t,w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>V=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and edge weights </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t(u,v)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> given by context-conditioned co-occurrence or attention.  
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the (normalized) Laplacian and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-s</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the heat kernel at scale </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Recurrent Community]
