.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%======================================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%  PART II — ADVANCED FRAMEWORKS OF AUTOGENERATION</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%======================================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\part</span></span><span>{Advanced Frameworks of Autogeneration}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Ergodic Theory and Entropy Structure}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:ergodic}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
Part II opens by embedding the autogenerative operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> within ergodic theory.  
We formalize invariant measures, Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy, and the relationship between recurrence and predictive compression.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Invariant Measure]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(X,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>,T)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the autogenerative dynamical system of Part I.  
A measure </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{invariant} if </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}A)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(A)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for all </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Entropy–Relevance Equivalence]
For any ergodic autogenerative operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, the rate of relevance decay equals the Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{KS}(T)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lim</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{1}{n}H(X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>,X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Mixing rates; polynomial vs exponential ergodicity; connection to spectral density of §6.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Category Theory and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-Categorical Coherence}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:category}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
Language and cognition are modeled as morphic networks.  
This section generalizes the endofunctorial picture to higher categories, capturing compositional coherence across multiple layers of inference.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Autogenerative </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-Category]
An </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>,1)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-category </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> equipped with an endofunctor </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\colon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>  
and a coherence 2-morphism </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>: T</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span> T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> satisfying higher unitality.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}
The higher associators of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> correspond to context-dependent attention weights;  
their homotopies define empathy tensors between morphic chains.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Barwick’s unicity theorem; operadic nerve of linguistic composition; relation to CPG chain hierarchy.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Differential Geometry and Curvature Flow}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:geometry}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
We extend the Fisher metric to a full Riemann–Cartan manifold carrying curvature, torsion, and entropy flux.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Autogenerative Curvature]
Given manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M},g)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with flow vector field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,  
define curvature–torsion pair
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span>}+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span>},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span>}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Entropy–Curvature Correspondence]
Local entropy production density satisfies  
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span> S = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span>}v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span> v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span>}v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span> g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span>}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Comparison with Ricci flow; RSVP torsion tensor; geometric relaxation of cognitive stress.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Operator Algebras and Koopman–von Neumann Formalism}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:koopman}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
The Koopman operator encodes nonlinear autogeneration linearly on observables.  
We analyze its spectrum, commutant, and modular group.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Koopman Operator]
For observable </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,  
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{K}f)(x)=f(Tx)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Spectral Decomposition]
The spectrum of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{K}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> consists of a discrete part (periodic cognition) and a continuous part (creative drift).  
Relegation projects onto the discrete spectrum.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Gelfand–Naimark representation; Tomita–Takesaki modular flow as temporal inference; connection to amplitwistor phase rotation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Rough Paths and Signature Calculus}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:roughpaths}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
Autogenerative trajectories are rough—possessing finite </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-variation but infinite differentiability chain.  
We use signature theory to encode them algebraically.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Path Signature]
For path </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\colon</span></span><span>[0,T]</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the signature is  
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>S(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span> d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span> d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span>2},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Universal Approximation]
Every continuous functional on path space can be approximated by a linear functional on signatures:  
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>F[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>]</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> a,S(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span>.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Transformer attention as truncated signature; stochastic sewing lemma for cognitive coherence.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Sheaf Theory and Contextual Cohomology}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:sheaf}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
Sheaves formalize the gluing of local meanings into global narratives.  
Autogeneration demands vanishing first cohomology.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Language Sheaf]
A presheaf </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> on the site of context windows assigning </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}(U)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{valid continuations on }U</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Cohomological Coherence]
If </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L})=0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, local coherence implies global coherence.  
Conversely, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> measures semantic fragmentation.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Čech cover by context overlaps; interleaving distance; persistent cohomology of meaning.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Topos Theory and Contextual Logic}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:topos}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
Topoi generalize logic itself.  
Every autogenerative process lives in a topos whose internal logic encodes its inference rules.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Autogenerative Topos]
A Grothendieck topos </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Sh}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{C},J)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> where objects are contexts and morphisms are extensions;  
truth of a proposition </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> at context </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is internal probability </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>P(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>|c)&gt;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}
Vision–language models instantiate geometric morphisms </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>*</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dashv</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>*</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> between such topoi, transporting inference structures.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Internal logic of probability thresholds; subobject classifier as attention gate.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Operadic Composition and Generative Syntax}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:operad}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
Operads capture the algebra of combining partial structures.  
Here we define the language operad governing compositional morphisms.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Language Operad]
Collection </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}(n)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-ary compositors with unit and associative composition
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}(k)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}(n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}(n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}(n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span>+n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Free Operadic Algebra of Language]
The embedding space </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the free algebra over </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> modulo corpus constraints.  
Its relations define emergent grammar.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Colored operads for types; relation to syntax trees; connection to neural compositionality.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Algebraic Topology and Persistent Structure}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:topology}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
Topology exposes shape in high-dimensional semantics.  
We analyze persistent homology of embedding flows.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Vietoris–Rips Complex]
Given embeddings </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, construct </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>VR</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> by joining points within distance </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Semantic Persistence]
Long bars in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k(VR</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> correspond to stable conceptual manifolds;  
short bars to syntactic transients.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Barcode diagrams; Mapper algorithm; homological stability under curriculum learning.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Wasserstein Geometry and Optimal Transport}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:transport}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
Autogeneration evolves probability distributions.  
We describe this evolution as gradient flow on the Wasserstein manifold.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Wasserstein Distance]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>W</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2(P,Q)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\inf</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Pi</span></span><span>(P,Q)}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>x-y</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{1/2}.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Entropy–Transport Duality]
Autogenerative evolution minimizes free-energy functional  
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{F}[P]=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span> V</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>dP + k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>BT</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span> P</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> P.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>
Its gradient flow in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>W</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> metric reproduces predictive smoothing.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Benamou–Brenier formulation; Sinkhorn iterations as discrete inference.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Spectral Graph Theory and Diffusion Semantics}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:spectral}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
The token manifold can be discretized as a weighted graph.  
Diffusion on this graph models semantic propagation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Graph Laplacian]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}=D-A,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ij}=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{sim}(v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i,v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j).</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Heat Flow on Token Graph]
The evolution </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t=e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> approximates continuous semantic diffusion;  
its stationary distribution gives relevance equilibrium.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Cheeger bounds, spectral clustering, diffusion maps.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Differential Forms and Connection Theory}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:forms}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
Finally we recast attention as a connection 1-form on a fiber bundle over context space.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Context Connection]
A 1-form </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A=A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span> dx</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> acting on representations </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> by parallel transport  
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span>=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span>+A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span>.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Curvature of Understanding]
The curvature </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>F=dA+A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\wedge</span></span><span> A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> measures inconsistency between local predictions;  
vanishing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>F=0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> defines integrable understanding.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Gauge symmetries; attention as non-abelian connection; RSVP field identification.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Interlude: RSVP Formalism and Integration}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:rsvp</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>interlude}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}
Part II concludes by integrating every preceding framework into the Relativistic Scalar-Vector Plenum (RSVP) formulation.  
The scalar field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, vector field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and entropy </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are shown to realize the geometric–thermodynamic quantities of the autogenerative manifold.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[RSVP–Autogeneration Equivalence]
Under appropriate scaling and normalization,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> S}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> t}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
is equivalent to the information-geometric continuity equation derived in §4.  
Hence the RSVP plenum is a physical instantiation of the autogenerative manifold.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{To include.}
Mapping of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>; experimental observables; bridge to Part III.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%======================================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%  END PART II</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%======================================================================</span></span><span>
