<hr data-start="2406" data-end="2409">
<h3 data-start="2411" data-end="2429">✅ How it works</h3>
<ol data-start="2431" data-end="2897">
<li data-start="2431" data-end="2530">
<p data-start="2434" data-end="2530"><strong data-start="2434" data-end="2449">Your Action</strong> already copies <code data-start="2465" data-end="2480">manifest.yaml</code> and <code data-start="2485" data-end="2512">autogenerative_thesis.pdf</code> into <code data-start="2518" data-end="2527">public/</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2531" data-end="2579">
<p data-start="2534" data-end="2579">GitHub Pages serves those files statically.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2580" data-end="2730">
<p data-start="2583" data-end="2730">When a browser loads the page, the small script fetches <code data-start="2639" data-end="2654">manifest.yaml</code>, extracts <code data-start="2665" data-end="2674">version</code>, <code data-start="2676" data-end="2685">updated</code>, and <code data-start="2691" data-end="2703">maintainer</code>, and populates the page.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2731" data-end="2897">
<p data-start="2734" data-end="2897">Whenever you push a new commit, the workflow overwrites the manifest with the latest date and version, so the web page automatically reflects it—no manual editing.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="2899" data-end="2902">
