t:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="186">Yes — that’s straightforward.<br data-start="29" data-end="32">
To make a reproducible repository you can upload directly to GitHub (and also build locally with one command), you want a single <code data-start="161" data-end="167">.zip</code> bundle containing:</p>
<ul data-start="188" data-end="456">
<li data-start="188" data-end="263">
<p data-start="190" data-end="263">all <strong data-start="194" data-end="216">LaTeX source files</strong> (<code data-start="218" data-end="229">part1.tex</code>, <code data-start="231" data-end="242">part2.tex</code>, <code data-start="244" data-end="254">main.tex</code>, etc.)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="264" data-end="336">
<p data-start="266" data-end="336"><strong data-start="266" data-end="302">Python figure-generation scripts</strong> (for your simulations or plots)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="337" data-end="379">
<p data-start="339" data-end="379"><strong data-start="339" data-end="356">shell scripts</strong> (to build and clean)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="380" data-end="456">
<p data-start="382" data-end="456"><strong data-start="382" data-end="406">CI/CD infrastructure</strong> (Dockerfile, workflow YAML, manifest, index.html)</p>
</li>
</ul>
