</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\newcommand</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\given</span></span><span>}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\middle</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ---------- Part header ----------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{center}
{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\LARGE</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bfseries</span></span><span> Part I — The Autogenerative Thesis:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>[2pt]
From Non-Markovian Language to Cognitive Geometry}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>[0.5em]
{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\large</span></span><span> Flyxion Research Group}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{center}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\setcounter</span></span><span>{section}{0}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Introduction: The Autogenerative Hypothesis}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:intro}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Thesis.}
Natural computation---language, perception, planning---is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{autogenerative}: the present encodes its own lawful continuation. Unlike finite-order Markov assumptions, autogeneration is intrinsically </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{non-Markovian}: predictive content depends on the whole </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{trajectory} (not just the last </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> tokens) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{barenholtz2025autoregression}. The corpus is therefore a field of continuation rules whose internal geometry constrains how thought flows.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Autogenerative system]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be a token/feature space and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the set of finite histories. An </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{autogenerative system} is a pair </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(X,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\TT</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\TT</span></span><span>:X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span>(X)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a continuation operator inferred solely from sequential data (no external grammar), and the induced conditional </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>P(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t})=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\TT</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> exhibits irreducible long memory (Def.~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{def:irreducible-long-memory}).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\noindent</span></span><span> This Part develops the analytic backbone of autogeneration across six lenses: measure theory and filtrations (Sec.~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:measure}), information geometry (Sec.~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:info-geom}), categorical dynamics (Sec.~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:category}), topological persistence and rough paths (Sec.~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:topo}), operator–spectral formalism (Sec.~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:operator}), and thermodynamic/variational analogies (Sec.~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:thermo}). We end with a synthesis (Sec.~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:synthesis}), historical context (Sec.~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:history}), and a compact mathematical summary (Sec.~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:summary}), completing Part I.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{figure}[H]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\centering</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\fbox</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\parbox</span></span><span>{0.92</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\linewidth</span></span><span>}{
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Figure 1. Autogeneration vs.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>finite-</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> Markovism.}
Left: </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-Markov closure truncates context; curvature of meaning is local and myopic.
Right: autogeneration integrates the whole past trajectory; curvature accumulates and guides continuation.
The shaded ribbon represents uncertainty; its thickness shrinks along regions of high geometric coherence.
}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\caption</span></span><span>{Conceptual contrast that motivates non-Markovian modeling.}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{fig:autogen-vs-markov}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{figure}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Mathematical Framework of Autogenerative Systems}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:framework}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>:X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\RR</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be an encoder; </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> defines a path </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>:[0,T]</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\RR</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> by interpolation. Continuations depend on geometry of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:nonlocal}
P(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:t})=g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span> h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ddot</span></span><span> h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Energy functional and geodesic continuation]
Given a Riemannian metric </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> on representation space, define
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:energy}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>]=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dd</span></span><span> t + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>T V(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>(t))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dd</span></span><span> t,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
with contextual potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>V</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Minimizers are </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{geodesic continuations} that balance smoothness and constraint adaptation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{amari1985,friston2010}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[No finite-</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> suffices]</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{prop:no-finite-k}
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>V</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are such that distinct histories induce distinct jets </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span> h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>,h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(m)})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for all fixed </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then no fixed-window statistic is sufficient: for all </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k&lt;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
