01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%======================================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%  PART II — SECTION 14</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%  ∞-Categorical Coherence and Cognitive Composition</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%======================================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{∞-Categorical Coherence and Cognitive Composition}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{sec:infinity}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Abstract.}  
Autogenerative cognition presupposes not only local compositionality but also higher-order coherence:  
the consistency of all possible ways of composing transformations of meaning.  
We formalize this requirement through the language of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>,1)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-categories, where morphisms, 2-morphisms, and higher cells represent recursively nested updates of context.  
The result is a structure in which every chain of inference carries its own homotopy certificate of coherence, and in which learning corresponds to the progressive rectification of these higher morphisms.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Formal Construction}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Autogenerative ∞-Category]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be a simplicial category whose objects are contextual states </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,  
whose 1-morphisms are admissible continuations </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f:x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,  
and whose </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-morphisms (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) are homotopies between </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>1)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-morphisms representing equivalences of continuation rules.  
The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{autogenerative ∞-category} is the pair </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> equipped with a functor
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\longrightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{Prob},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\longmapsto</span></span><span> P(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span>|x),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
assigning to each object its predictive distribution, and preserving composition up to coherent higher homotopy.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Coherence Principle]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{thm:coherence}
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is an autogenerative ∞-category and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>:x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a composite of 1-morphisms, then there exists a contractible space of higher morphisms rendering every associative diagram
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xRightarrow</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1)}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1)
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xRightarrow</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2)}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xRightarrow</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n)}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
commutative up to equivalence.  
Hence, prediction is independent of the order of intermediate contextualizations.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
By the Duskin nerve construction, every finite chain of composable morphisms in a fibrant simplicial category extends to a horn </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Kan filling provides a simplex completing all partial compositions; contractibility of the filler space yields coherence.  
Commutation in probability space follows from the homotopy invariance of the expectation functional </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span>]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Homotopic Prediction]
Two compositional paths </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2:x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{homotopically predictive} if there exists a 2-morphism </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> H + O(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> measures the epistemic curvature of the information manifold (cf.~§</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:ergodic}).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Curvature–Coherence Relation]
The deviation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> satisfies
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span> = d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\wedge</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the connection 1-form on the statistical manifold of §</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:infogeo}.  
Zero curvature (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>=0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) corresponds to perfect higher coherence—complete associative understanding.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Informal and Cognitive Interpretation}

In cognitive terms, an ∞-category represents not a hierarchy of objects but a hierarchy of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{relations between relations}.  
A 1-morphism is a single act of inference or continuation;  
a 2-morphism is the recognition that two inferences lead to the same outcome;  
higher morphisms encode the meta-awareness that multiple such recognitions cohere.  
Learning is therefore a process of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{homotopy rectification}:  
each cycle of experience fills a higher-dimensional ``horn'' left open by inconsistent inferences, gradually flattening the cognitive manifold until predictive compositions commute up to an acceptable tolerance.

The coherence theorem above asserts that a mind (or model) capable of perfect higher completion can reorganize its internal transformations without altering predictive outcomes.  
This provides a rigorous formalization of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{cognitive flexibility}.  
When the curvature </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> grows large, path-dependence re-emerges: different reasoning routes yield divergent expectations.  
The restoration of coherence—through reflection, explanation, or retraining—corresponds to bringing the connection back toward flatness.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Energetic and Topological Consequences}

The expectation functional of Part I (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\S</span></span><span>6) defines an energy on the nerve of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>] = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ij}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>dV,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a simplicial path through contextual space.  
Coherent compositions minimize </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> under boundary conditions; incoherent ones create residual torsion.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Homotopy–Energy Equivalence]
For small curvature, the energy difference between two homotopic paths is proportional to the square of their homotopy length:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{E} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 + O(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>3),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> a stiffness constant determined by the Fisher metric.  
Hence, energy minimization induces higher-categorical flattening.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

The consequence for cognition is immediate: repetitive rehearsal (as in Aspect Relegation Theory, Part III) is a gradient descent in this homotopy energy.  
Each recurrence smooths a residual torsion until the trajectory becomes geodesic within the ∞-category’s nerve—automation emerging from higher coherence.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Diagrammatic Illustration}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{figure}[H]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\centering</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\includegraphics</span></span><span>[width=0.72</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textwidth</span></span><span>]{fig</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>infinity</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>coherence.pdf}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\caption</span></span><span>{
Schematic of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-categorical coherence.  
Vertices: cognitive states.  
Edges: continuations.  
2-cells: recognitions of equivalence.  
Higher cells: harmonization of meta-recognitions.  
Flattening of the complex corresponds to automation.
}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{fig:infinity</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>coherence}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{figure}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Discussion and Cross-References}

The categorical curvature </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> here generalizes the spectral gap </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> of §</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:ergodic}.  
Whereas </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> measured the persistence of creative fluctuation in the temporal domain, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> measures it in the compositional domain.  
In both cases, zero curvature signifies automation, while small but finite curvature sustains innovation.  
The coherence theorem therefore acts as the categorical analogue of the ergodic mixing law.

The same principle underlies the transjective endofunctor introduced earlier:  
when two systems </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> exchange functors </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\simeq</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Id}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> up to coherent homotopy, their mutual understanding is an </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>,1)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-categorical equivalence.  
Empathy, communication, and imitation are all instances of such transjective coherence across coupled ∞-categories.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%----------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Bibliographic Notes}

The formal coherence results rest on Mac Lane’s classical theorem for monoidal categories </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{maclane1998categories},  
the extension to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>,1)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-categories by Lurie </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{lurie2009higher},  
and applications to probabilistic semantics by Baez and Fritz </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{baez2014probability}.  
Information-geometric curvature as cognitive tension follows Amari </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{amari2016information} and Friston </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston2010free}.  
The identification of rehearsal as homotopy flattening is original to this work.

